---
layout: changelog
title: "Use any S3 client with Appwrite Storage"
date: 2026-09-03
cover: /images/blog/announcing-s3-api/cover.avif
---

Appwrite Storage now exposes an **S3-compatible API**. Point the AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, rclone, and other S3 tools at your Appwrite buckets by changing the endpoint, credentials, and region.

Your project ID is the access key ID, and an API key secret is the secret access key. Existing buckets and files stay in place: objects uploaded over S3 appear in the Console, and files created through the native API are addressable over S3 by the same name. Multipart uploads work with the standard S3 operations.

You can also sign a **presigned URL** on your server and hand it to a client that has no Appwrite credentials. It authorizes one operation on one object for up to 7 days, and the endpoint answers cross-origin requests, so it works straight from a browser.

